            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>A common pain point has emerged among overseas teams recently: AI-generated ad copies are "clashing" far too often. Scrolling through Meta, TikTok, and Google Ads, similar products often feature nearly identical promotional copy. Users are getting fatigued, and both CTR and conversion rates are dropping.</p>
<p>Here are 5 high-frequency questions with direct answers and actionable plans.</p>
<h2>Question 1: Why Does AI-Generated Copy Tend to "Clash"?</h2>
<p>There's one core reason: most AI tools operate on the same logic and data sources.</p>
<p>When a "low-price promotion" or "limited-time offer" copy performs well on a platform, AI treats this pattern as high-weight reference material. The result? You use it, competitors use it, and the generated copy naturally converges.</p>
<p>Another reason is overly generic prompts. When your instructions to AI are just "write an attractive promotional copy," AI can only deliver generic templates.</p>
<p><strong>Verdict:</strong> If your prompts lack specific scenarios, target audiences, or differentiated selling points, AI-generated copy will likely "wear the same clothes" as competitors.</p>
<h2>Question 2: How to Tell If Your Copy Has Become Homogenized?</h2>
<p>Two actions:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Export your last 30 days of copy and calculate repetition rate</strong>. Ad management backends generally support exports. Use formulas in Excel to calculate similarity—anything over 20% needs adjustment.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Ask yourself three questions</strong>:</p>
</li>
<li>Does my copy start and end with exclamation marks and numbers?</li>
<li>Can this copy be used directly by changing a competitor's name?</li>
<li>Can users tell me apart from competitors after reading it?</li>
</ol>
<p>If two or more answers are "yes," homogenization has occurred.</p>
<h2>Question 3: How to Adjust AI Copy for Differentiation?</h2>
<p><strong>Direct answer: AI generation is the starting point, not the endpoint.</strong></p>
<p>Practical steps:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Step 1</strong>: Have AI generate 3 initial drafts from different angles (functional, emotional, situational).</li>
<li><strong>Step 2</strong>: Manually select one and supplement it with your own product details and user insights.</li>
<li><strong>Step 3</strong>: Feed this revised copy back to AI and ask it to generate 5 variants.</li>
<li><strong>Step 4</strong>: Pick the variant that looks least like a "standard template."</li>
</ul>
<p>A utility app team tested this: AI-generated copy directly used achieved a 1.2% CTR, while manually refined copy reached 2.8%. That's the value of human judgment.</p>
<h2>Question 4: What Are the Core Elements of Differentiated Copy?</h2>
<p>Three elements, ranked by importance:</p>
<p><strong>1. Specific Pain Points, Not Generic Selling Points</strong></p>
<p>❌ "Efficient financial management, freeing your hands"<br />
✅ "Reconciliations always wrong? Get last month's finances sorted in 3 minutes"</p>
<p><strong>2. Consistent Brand Voice</strong></p>
<p>A young trendy brand and a premium business brand should use completely different tones even when selling the same headphones. AI doesn't know who you are—you need to tell it.</p>
<p><strong>3. Situational Expression</strong></p>
<p>Specific scenarios like "during commute," "late-night overtime," or "weekend parenting" create more recognition than vague phrases like "anytime, anywhere" or "essential for life."</p>
<h2>Question 5: How Can Operators Maintain Creative Competitiveness in the AI Era?</h2>
<p>Simple answer: <strong>Treat AI as an assistant, not a brain.</strong></p>
<p>Specific approaches:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Build your own "copy arsenal"</strong>: Collect real language from user comments, customer service conversations, and social media interactions—these are differentiated materials AI hasn't been trained on.</li>
<li><strong>Mandatory: test at least 1 purely human-written copy per campaign</strong>: Not to prove humans are better than AI, but to maintain sensitivity to user language.</li>
<li><strong>Spend 30 minutes weekly browsing competitor ads</strong>: No complex tools needed, just use ad libraries or scroll through platforms. Screenshot overly homogenized copy as negative examples—these are more useful than positive cases.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>AI has lowered the barrier to copy production, but increased the difficulty of differentiation. When everyone can use AI to produce quickly, <strong>depth of understanding user needs and quality of human judgment</strong> become the true competitive edge.</p>
<p>Recommend teams turn these 5 questions into an internal checklist. Running through it before each new copy launch can reduce "collisions" by at least half.</p>
